WEBVTT

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:48.450
Midwinter, and the countryside is so still, it seems almost lifeless.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:56.260
But these trees and bushes and grasses around me are living organisms, just like animals.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.970
And they have to face very much the same sort of...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.110
of problems as animals face throughout their lives if they're to survive they have to fight one

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:14.740
another they have to compete for mates they have to invade new territories but the reason that we

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:20.900
are seldom aware of these dramas is that plants of course live on a different time scale but

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:29.680
these days we have ways of speeding things up visually

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:34.750
And then you can see just how dramatic the lives of plants can be.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:48.200
Condense three months into 20 seconds and the desolation of winter quickly warms into the riot of spring.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:59.970
Speed a week into a minute and you can sense the urgency with which the ground living...

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.680
plants race to unfurl their flowers.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:43.700
Wood anemones nod attentively at the sun as it rises and sets each day.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:55.460
Above, hazel leaves moving to the same rhythm pulse as they expand to their full size.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:29.880
beneath them the broad leaves of docks are rising from the ground foxgloves gape almost

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:41.600
alarmingly as they invite insects to come and collect their pollen. Strange

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.850
though it may seem, some plants can move not just their flowers and their

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:52.330
leaves but they can travel from place to place. Take for example this bramble.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:07.710
Of all the woodland plants, this is one of the most aggressive.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:15.110
It waves its shoots agitatedly from side to side,

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:18.000
as if feeling for the best way forward.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:26.990
And when a shoot settles on its course, it thrusts ahead relentlessly.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:50.300
Invading stems, backward-pointing spines give it the grip it needs to climb over almost anything that stands in its way.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:04.690
It can advance as much as three inches in a day.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:13.960
Before long, the shoot will put down rootlets,

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:18.460
and then new territory will have been annexed for the Bramble's empire.

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:26.490
Other adult plants travel even faster.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.620
But the desert dunes are always moving,

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:38.400
and a sheltered site can suddenly become intolerably exposed.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.200
So the plant must find a new place.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.570
is now dead, but within it.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.020
There is still life.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:37.040
These tiny particles are the next generation.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.140
Each is a miracle of packaging

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.980
because each, after all, contains complete genetic instructions

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:47.160
for rebuilding an adult plant like this.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.980
And it's precisely because these grains are so small

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:55.120
that it is in this form that most plants do most of their travelling.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:58.710
Some of these genetic particles, in fact, are microscopic.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:03.740
The smallest of all belong to fungi.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.420
Fungi are not, to be accurate, plants at all.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:09.630
They belong to a kingdom all their own.

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.780
But the particles they produce, called spores,

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:15.160
are in many ways similar to seeds.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.380
A single puffball produces so many

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:25.380
that someone has calculated that if, for two generations,

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.520
every spore grew into an adult,

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.780
the resultant mass of puffballs...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.200
would be 800 times the volume of the earth.

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:40.080
Like the birdcage plant, a puffball can be carried along by the wind.

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:43.810
But the real long-distance travelling is done by the spores

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:46.770
that are knocked from it in clouds, like smoke.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:07:59.980
In autumn...

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:07.680
Other smaller fungi appear on the woodland floor, earth stars.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:14.210
Their appearance, just after they've emerged above ground,

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.890
gives little hint of how complex they will become.

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.820
As the damp autumn airs blow through the leafless woods,

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:24.440
the earth stars begin to transform themselves.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:13.230
air to take advantage of the falling rain. A drip gives them all the energy they need

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:15.850
to propel their spores into the air.

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:39.860
Flowers also use the wind to transport their seeds, and few do it more successfully than dandelions.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:59.890
As their petals fall, their flower heads, over a period of one or two weeks, are transformed.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.540
into wonderfully intricate globes,

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:05.760
each a precise array of 100 or so seeds,

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:07.920
all awaiting the wind.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:18.880
Their seeds are much heftier than the spores of fungi.

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:22.520
For them to fly, special apparatus is needed.

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:28.900
Each is fitted with its own individual parachute.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:41.070
The device is so efficient that a breeze will carry the seeds high into the sky.

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:52.080
In such a dense crowd of adult plants as this, there's no room for the next generation.

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:56.800
The seeds must get away, and the wind will take them for miles.

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:29.790
it is being pulled

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.600
advantage when dispatching their seeds by air, their height. The farther a seed has to fall,

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:41.980
the longer it takes to reach the ground and the further it can travel. And these cottonwood trees

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:56.080
need do no more than provide their seeds with straightforward fluff. This, because of their

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:58.890
height, is enough to carry them for miles.

00:12:04.348 --> 00:12:05.008
Every summer.

00:12:05.372 --> 00:12:09.172
the waters of the great lakes of North America become thickly flecked

00:12:09.172 --> 00:12:11.852
with seeds from the cottonwoods growing around their margins.

00:12:12.796 --> 00:12:15.796
A few will wash up on a distant shore and germinate.

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:17.580
Most will be lost.

00:12:18.172 --> 00:12:20.672
But the cottonwoods produce seeds in such numbers.

00:12:20.956 --> 00:12:23.196
that the loss is of no consequence whatever.

00:12:27.036 --> 00:12:29.516
There is much less wind in the tropical rainforest.

00:12:30.252 --> 00:12:33.432
Even above the canopy, the humid air hangs as a mist.

00:12:33.996 --> 00:12:37.296
And below, there is seldom even a breath in the air.

00:12:37.996 --> 00:12:40.136
If a plant is to exploit its height here,

00:12:40.516 --> 00:12:43.976
it has to give its seeds very good flying equipment indeed.

00:12:44.556 --> 00:12:45.896
And none does that better.

00:12:46.188 --> 00:12:47.828
than this liana in Borneo.

00:13:10.876 --> 00:13:13.376
Aircraft designers have tried to build a wing.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:14.920
as efficient as this one.

00:13:15.356 --> 00:13:15.976
but failed.

00:13:16.796 --> 00:13:20.276
even the faintest updraft produced by the slightest thermal.

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:23.900
is enough to lift this little glider with its seed passenger.

00:13:24.412 --> 00:13:26.152
and so extend its flight.

00:13:57.196 --> 00:13:59.996
France preceded humanity in building...

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.720
fixed-wing gliders, and not only gliders.

00:14:03.452 --> 00:14:05.252
They created helicopters, too.

00:14:10.972 --> 00:14:12.012
Sycamore seeds.

00:14:28.764 --> 00:14:29.964
The balance between

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.400
the weight of the seed and the length and width of the wing is exact and perfect.

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:39.660
A slightly heavier seed or a shorter and narrower wing

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:42.560
and the whole thing would fall like a stone.

00:14:45.644 --> 00:14:49.884
The tri-star plant produces a revolving seed with six blades.

00:14:50.348 --> 00:14:52.588
and aircraft designers have yet to...

00:14:52.592 --> 00:14:53.592
I'll copy that.

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:01.900
Or this.

00:15:13.852 --> 00:15:15.852
The plants also use explosives.

00:15:24.668 --> 00:15:25.548
Jet Propulsion.

00:15:30.668 --> 00:15:32.668
This is a squirting cucumber.

00:15:41.612 --> 00:15:42.152
And this.

00:15:43.468 --> 00:15:44.528
Himalayan balsam.

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:51.260
Its seed capsules are pumped full of liquid to such a pressure.

00:15:51.532 --> 00:15:53.772
that the slightest touch makes them explode.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:11.320
The force is so great that seeds can be shot away for as much as 15 feet.

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:29.250
Miesembryanthemum seed heads are opened by rain.

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:38.260
It's the sudden absorption of water by their dried tissues that powers their opening.

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:43.180
Once open, they expose a screen as taut as a trampoline.

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:49.840
Raindrops bounce off it, taking the seeds with them.

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:05.700
Water provides many plants with the power they need for travelling.

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:08.770
Provided the seeds are given the right equipment,

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:11.840
it can shift really heavy, bulky ones.

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:15.390
Beside many a tropical river,

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:19.680
there hangs the biggest of all seed pods, the sea bean.

00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:25.190
These huge containers house one of the most successful

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:27.310
of all vegetable travellers.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.520
There's a groove across the pod between each seed

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:37.400
so that each can fall away independently in its own separate packaging.

00:17:43.530 --> 00:17:46.770
One by one, the sea beans start on their voyages.

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:50.960
This one is setting off down a small river in Africa.

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:22.980
After a few miles, even perhaps a few hundred miles,

00:18:23.320 --> 00:18:25.620
the seed arrives at the mouth of its river

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:28.750
and makes its way through the mangroves to the sea.

00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:42.350
It can voyage through groups of islands and out into the open sea

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:46.030
to ride the great ocean currents for as much as a year

00:18:46.030 --> 00:18:47.660
and still remain alive.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:59.580
Its protective packaging may become so frayed and tattered

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:02.660
that it disintegrates and releases the seed.

00:19:05.540 --> 00:19:09.830
But even this is no disaster, for the seed is able to float by itself.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:15.060
Many, doubtless, are lost at sea,

00:19:15.560 --> 00:19:19.420
but some eventually reach another, and maybe a distant, coast.

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:27.520
This one has landed on a tropical beach in Northern Australia.

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:29.460
I've no idea where it came from.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.180
could be from a tree just a few miles up the coast or it could be from another

00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:39.300
continent. Sea beans are the great success story of seed distribution. Every

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:43.000
year they land on the coasts of Europe having been bought there by the Gulf

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:47.600
Stream from the Caribbean. Of course it's too cold from there and they seldom

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:51.920
germinate but provided they land in the tropics they will almost certainly grow.

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:54.870
There's one standing at the head of this very beach.

00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:10.040
So, some plants send their seeds by sea, some by air, but most, in fact, use living couriers.

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:21.040
Animals with hairy coats are easily conscripted.

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:29.980
The burdock uses hook.

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:39.210
Hundreds of them, and very effective they are.

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:56.610
Eventually, the burrs are licked off, picked off, or shaken off.

00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:59.820
And if the burdock is lucky, that will happen someday.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.780
from where the adult plant grew.

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:13.800
Trousers will serve just as well as hairy coats,

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:16.300
and shoes as hooves or paws.

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:21.160
Here in Southern Africa, there's a creeper that uses not hooks but spikes,

00:21:21.700 --> 00:21:23.200
waiting for you to tread on them.

00:21:25.750 --> 00:21:29.060
These things are called by the local people.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:36.160
devil thorns, and you can quite see why. If you trot on that with your naked foot, whether

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:43.370
you're human or animal, it must be very painful indeed. But if you think that's bad, what

00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:43.870
about this?

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:14.260
This is the seed case of the grapple plant.

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:23.120
Animals with cleft hooves or relatively soft pads

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:25.400
can be crippled by the grapple plant.

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:29.620
But the bony, scaly feet of ostrich are particularly...

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:49.120
tough. They can carry this vicious hitchhiker for many miles without any ill effects. But

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:54.280
there's another way of treating messengers. Instead of relying on chance encounters with

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:57.260
them, you can entice them with tempting rewards.

00:23:00.350 --> 00:23:05.390
Ants are indefatigable workers, and many plants in the dry heathlands of southern Africa

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:11.470
engage them as carriers by attaching a small reward of ant food to the end of their seeds.

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:21.750
If a seed lies out in the open for long here, a mouse or some other rodent will eat it.

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:27.670
If it's to survive, it must get below ground quickly, and the ants take it there.

00:23:41.870 --> 00:23:44.210
The fleshy bit at the end is all the ants want.

00:23:44.570 --> 00:23:46.210
The rest is of no interest to them.

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:49.440
So the seed has now reached a safe resting place

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:51.440
and an ideal position to germinate

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:53.320
just below the surface of the ground.

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:57.300
Plants bribe larger animals too, including us.

00:23:58.350 --> 00:23:59.970
And what's more, they force...

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.600
to fit in with their timetable.

00:24:02.876 --> 00:24:08.656
This blackberry, for example, is not yet ready for my services, so its fruits are still sour.

00:24:10.524 --> 00:24:14.764
But as the seeds complete their development, the flesh around them sweetens

00:24:14.764 --> 00:24:17.244
and announces the fact by changing colour.

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:29.620
The blackberry seeds will be more widely distributed if the plant is visited by a succession of different messengers.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.600
than if it's stripped on a single visit by just one.

00:24:32.908 --> 00:24:36.308
so its berries do not all ripen simultaneously.

00:24:44.588 --> 00:24:46.508
Birds find them irresistible.

00:24:47.052 --> 00:24:48.972
and quickly spot them for black.

00:24:49.196 --> 00:24:50.696
is a very conspicuous color.

00:24:52.748 --> 00:24:53.568
And so too.

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:56.060
is red.

00:25:07.548 --> 00:25:10.648
Birds see colour in very much the same way as we do.

00:25:10.972 --> 00:25:13.112
What is vivid to them is vivid to us.

00:25:13.788 --> 00:25:19.488
So rowans and ewes, strawberries and plums, cherries and hawthorns use red or black.

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:21.640
to summon birds to collect their fruit.

00:25:35.084 --> 00:25:38.764
Tropical figs produce much smaller fruit than their European relative

00:25:38.764 --> 00:25:42.164
and, unusually, as they ripen, they turn yellow.

00:25:42.924 --> 00:25:45.384
Even so, their message is widely understood.

00:25:47.084 --> 00:25:50.844
A tropical fig tree in fruit is a huge bonanza in the forest.

00:25:51.052 --> 00:25:53.292
All kinds of diners come to the tree.

00:26:03.292 --> 00:26:07.032
As well as fruit-eating birds, they attract all kinds of mammals,

00:26:07.512 --> 00:26:09.552
monkeys, squirrels and gibbons.

00:26:33.644 --> 00:26:38.184
The rhinoceros hornbill, in order to eat such small fruit with such a huge beak,

00:26:38.604 --> 00:26:40.324
has to be a bit of a juggler.

00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:51.940
And now comes the important part, as far as the fig is concerned.

00:26:52.556 --> 00:26:55.556
Dozens of fruits containing hundreds of seeds.

00:26:55.628 --> 00:26:57.868
I've fed it away in the hornbill's crop.

00:26:58.188 --> 00:26:59.188
for many miles.

00:27:00.092 --> 00:27:02.052
The flesh of the fruit will be digested.

00:27:02.428 --> 00:27:05.428
And then the indigestible bits, which includes the seeds,

00:27:05.748 --> 00:27:08.108
will be voided in a distant place in the forest.

00:27:10.332 --> 00:27:11.852
All forest canopies, however,

00:27:12.128 --> 00:27:16.928
do not have such a rich variety of fruit eaters to serve as messengers as there are in Borneo.

00:27:17.596 --> 00:27:18.316
In New Guinea.

00:27:18.684 --> 00:27:21.524
There are very few mammals and no monkeys at all.

00:27:22.172 --> 00:27:25.692
The biggest creature on the forest floor is not an antelope or a great ape.

00:27:26.172 --> 00:27:26.872
It's a bird.

00:27:29.468 --> 00:27:29.988
The castle.

00:27:33.132 --> 00:27:36.932
Both male and female casseries have vividly coloured wattles.

00:27:37.356 --> 00:27:41.156
and the similarity between them and the fruits which are the birds' main food.

00:27:41.484 --> 00:27:43.484
may not be merely coincidence.

00:27:48.524 --> 00:27:51.884
The wattles serve as social signals between the birds.

00:27:52.556 --> 00:27:57.916
Did plants exploit that by adopting the same colours to call the casser's attention to their fruit?

00:27:58.380 --> 00:27:59.640
Or was it the other way round?

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:04.120
Did the casseries make themselves more attractive to their mates by reminding them?

00:28:04.384 --> 00:28:05.144
of a good meal.

00:28:05.788 --> 00:28:07.128
No one can say for sure.

00:28:12.284 --> 00:28:13.084
What is certain

00:28:13.340 --> 00:28:16.900
is that casseries have no difficulty in finding objects with these colours.

00:28:17.468 --> 00:28:18.808
scattered among the leaves.

00:28:27.068 --> 00:28:29.468
Visual signals, however, have their limitations.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:31.000
advertisements.

00:28:31.148 --> 00:28:34.608
In thick forest, you just can't see them unless you're quite close.

00:28:35.340 --> 00:28:37.420
But there's another medium for messages.

00:28:38.156 --> 00:28:38.696
Smell.

00:28:39.276 --> 00:28:43.116
It may not be so precise, but it works over much greater distances.

00:28:46.188 --> 00:28:49.948
In Borneo, one fruit produces a smell so pungent.

00:28:50.284 --> 00:28:53.724
that a sensitive nose can detect it from half a mile away.

00:28:54.444 --> 00:28:57.164
And some people like the taste of that fruit so much

00:28:57.244 --> 00:28:59.484
that they will walk for miles to find it.

00:29:00.860 --> 00:29:04.540
And so will others.

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:28.970
This is it, the famous durian.

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:43.840
The smell, to my nostrils, at any rate, is fairly disgusting.

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:49.010
An open sewer with just a dash of coal gas would be a fair description.

00:29:49.250 --> 00:29:52.170
But that comes from the Rhine. That's the advertisement.

00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:58.420
This is the fruit, and that's very different.

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:02.480
but it's really pretty good.

00:30:02.580 --> 00:30:08.380
Kind of slimy, caramel cream perhaps

00:30:08.380 --> 00:30:09.980
would be a description of this.

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:24.840
But if all Europeans aren't instantly Durian addicts, all orangutans are.

00:31:16.732 --> 00:31:20.492
Animals may carry seeds for long distances in their stomachs.

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:23.140
but most get rid of them more or less at random.

00:31:24.060 --> 00:31:25.060
Some plants...

00:31:25.116 --> 00:31:26.776
that is simply not good enough.

00:31:34.156 --> 00:31:39.016
The fruits of the truia tree that grow in the forests of Nepal have a particular problem.

00:31:39.660 --> 00:31:43.140
their seeds cannot germinate and grow in deep shade.

00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:47.880
They have to be taken out of the forest into a clearing if they're to stand a chance.

00:31:48.332 --> 00:31:51.032
And there is one big animal that will do that for them.

00:31:52.876 --> 00:31:53.336
The Great.

00:31:53.552 --> 00:31:54.892
Indian Minosolus.

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:07.040
It's so fond of these fruits that they're called locally rhino apples.

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:18.030
The rhinos usually feed in the forest during the heat of the day,

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:19.840
but in the cool of the evening,

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:23.800
they habitually move out onto the open grasslands that fringe the rivers.

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:47.720
The grasslands are created by monsoon floods

00:32:47.720 --> 00:32:50.780
that every few years wash away parts of the forest.

00:32:51.400 --> 00:32:53.660
Rhinos visit them for the rich grazing.

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:06.990
And out here, on regularly used communal middens,

00:33:07.290 --> 00:33:09.650
they perform the last of their daily duties.

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:20.290
And there, neatly deposited with a ration of fertilising manure,

00:33:20.730 --> 00:33:22.110
are the seeds of the truier.

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:29.960
So, young truiers sprout in open country...

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:31.300
on the rhino's dunghills.

00:33:31.940 --> 00:33:34.840
In due course, other trees will grow up around them

00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:37.460
and the forest will colonize the grasslands.

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:41.080
And then the truia fruit once more will have a problem

00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:44.410
and once more rely on the rhino to solve it.

00:33:46.210 --> 00:33:51.380
Sadly, this magnificent animal is getting rarer and rarer.

00:33:52.590 --> 00:33:54.850
Over millions of years of evolution,

00:33:55.250 --> 00:33:58.480
the truia tree has established a link with it.

00:33:58.940 --> 00:33:59.980
But if the great Indian...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:07.540
rhinoceros becomes extinct there's a great risk that the truia itself will disappear from the

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:24.880
grasslands and the riverbanks of southern nepal in africa elephants similarly have become crucial

00:34:24.880 --> 00:34:29.930
partners for acacias that may seem surprising for they're only two obvious

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:34.990
great destroyers of acacias, for when other food is short they will use their

00:34:34.990 --> 00:34:39.860
great bulk and strength to knock the trees down to eat their branches. But

00:34:39.860 --> 00:34:51.780
without elephants some species of acacia would barely survive. Each year acacias

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:55.900
produce huge crops of seeds and lots of animals come to feed on them.

00:35:03.800 --> 00:35:07.700
Inside the pods, the seeds are threatened by serious enemies.

00:35:08.680 --> 00:35:13.330
These, little beetle grubs that hatched from eggs injected into the pods.

00:35:13.940 --> 00:35:17.100
They will now eat all the seeds, unless they're stopped.

00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:27.520
Monkeys eat pods, seeds and grubs, chewing the whole lot very thoroughly.

00:35:28.230 --> 00:35:29.810
The acacia gets little benefit.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:31.420
from providing them with meals.

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:36.680
But elephants are different.

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:45.800
They greatly relish the seed pods, which are highly nutritious,

00:35:46.310 --> 00:35:49.790
and they go to considerable trouble to pick up what must be to them

00:35:49.790 --> 00:35:51.410
very fiddly little things.

00:35:51.430 --> 00:35:59.810
But they don't grind up their food into such a fine mash as monkeys.

00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:02.790
and having fed, they move on.

00:36:02.950 --> 00:36:20.960
They may well walk for several miles

00:36:20.960 --> 00:36:23.980
before, having digested their huge meals,

00:36:24.470 --> 00:36:25.650
they get rid of the remains.

00:36:40.840 --> 00:36:46.200
These acacia seeds have spent at least 24 hours inside an elephant's stomach.

00:36:46.480 --> 00:36:51.440
That hasn't harmed them, but it has killed stone dead those beetle grubs.

00:36:51.780 --> 00:36:56.730
In fact, the elephant's digestive juices have disinfected these seeds

00:36:56.730 --> 00:36:59.970
just as efficiently as a farmer does when he...

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.000
Dresses his seeds with insecticide.

00:37:13.620 --> 00:37:17.420
90% of acacia seeds in elephant dung germinate.

00:37:18.650 --> 00:37:20.970
Those in pods that are left uneaten on the ground

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:23.530
will nearly all be killed by beetle grunts.

00:37:24.100 --> 00:37:26.400
So acacia seeds eaten by an elephant

00:37:26.400 --> 00:37:28.300
have not merely been transported,

00:37:28.820 --> 00:37:29.980
they've been saved from...

00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:43.250
near certain death. Some seeds, however, are so well protected that it seems that nothing could

00:37:43.250 --> 00:37:50.610
eat them. These capsules, which in themselves are as hard as cannonballs, contain the individually

00:37:50.610 --> 00:37:53.880
armoured seeds, the nuts, of the Brazil nut tree.

00:38:18.620 --> 00:38:21.640
Even a fall of a couple of hundred feet doesn't crack them.

00:38:23.010 --> 00:38:29.220
Only one animal has the equipment to open them, the agouti.

00:38:46.480 --> 00:38:51.120
The agouti has two pairs of front teeth that are as sharp as chisels,

00:38:51.580 --> 00:38:55.740
and they enable it to gnaw a hole into the capsule and get at the seeds.

00:39:06.560 --> 00:39:09.680
But the Brazil nut tree has a way of protecting its seeds

00:39:09.680 --> 00:39:12.660
from the only animal that has managed to penetrate its armor.

00:39:12.980 --> 00:39:16.520
It presents it with 15 or 20 nuts,

00:39:17.110 --> 00:39:20.110
far more than an individual agouti could eat in one sitting.

00:39:21.860 --> 00:39:25.480
And the agouti has a habit which exactly suits the Brazil nut.

00:39:26.260 --> 00:39:29.960
It prudently buries what it can't eat immediately to save it for another.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:33.460
What it doesn't have is a perfect memory.

00:39:33.990 --> 00:39:35.890
It loses track of what it's buried

00:39:35.890 --> 00:39:39.420
and a significant proportion of the nuts survive to sprout.

00:39:43.000 --> 00:39:45.680
The alpine nutcracker, a kind of crow,

00:39:45.980 --> 00:39:49.320
is an even more obliging partner for the Arolla pine.

00:39:55.000 --> 00:39:57.620
The bird knows exactly how to open the cones

00:39:57.620 --> 00:39:59.440
and pick out the ripe seeds.

00:40:13.370 --> 00:40:16.370
Each one is swallowed, but it doesn't go into the stomach.

00:40:16.740 --> 00:40:20.020
It's stored in the crop while the bird tackles the next.

00:40:28.440 --> 00:40:29.970
And then, like the agouti,

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:34.790
the bird hides them, one by one, as provisions to help it through hard times.

00:40:39.150 --> 00:40:45.090
But unlike the agouti, it regularly carries the seeds away from the forest up onto open ground,

00:40:46.110 --> 00:40:52.050
perhaps because there it can more easily memorise landmarks to help it find the seeds again months later.

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:56.030
And some of these places suit the young trees very well.

00:41:07.360 --> 00:41:10.400
One by one, the bird brings them up from its crop.

00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:15.440
It buries them at just the right depth that suits the seed

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:21.710
and it carefully fills in the hole to conceal its treasure from other seed eaters.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:33.600
So the seeds of the Arolla pine are carried far from the parent tree

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:37.400
and planted with all the care that a human forester might give them,

00:41:37.640 --> 00:41:39.900
not only in high alpine meadows,

00:41:39.920 --> 00:41:43.710
but even in places which humans might find very difficult to reach,

00:41:43.950 --> 00:41:45.830
high up on the mountain ridges.

00:41:50.410 --> 00:41:54.470
Yet some plants succeed in reaching seemingly inaccessible sites

00:41:54.470 --> 00:41:59.290
without the help of any animal and entirely by their own exertions.

00:42:03.200 --> 00:42:07.340
A crack in a wall 50 feet above the ground is not easy to reach.

00:42:07.750 --> 00:42:11.690
But the ivy-leaved toad flax nonetheless manages to get there.

00:42:14.420 --> 00:42:18.020
It has no suckers like Virginia creeper to help it climb up here.

00:42:18.240 --> 00:42:20.320
No clinging roots like ivy.

00:42:20.950 --> 00:42:26.990
Its colonies manage to advance up the wall from crack to crack in an entirely different way.

00:42:28.860 --> 00:42:29.940
As the petals of its...

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:36.360
flowers fall off, the seeds within the capsule beneath begin to develop, and then the toad

00:42:36.360 --> 00:42:44.890
flax behaves in a most remarkable fashion. It finds the nearest crack and plants its seeds

00:42:44.910 --> 00:42:59.000
itself. So, one way or another, plants manage to get their seeds to the best places to germinate.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:03.780
If they have another question to settle, what is the best time to do so?

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:08.140
These proteas growing here on the southern tip of Africa

00:43:08.140 --> 00:43:13.580
have had their seeds ready and waiting inside the seed pods for several years now.

00:43:14.530 --> 00:43:15.730
Why haven't they released them?

00:43:16.420 --> 00:43:19.640
Well, the best moment to do so is about to arrive.

00:43:37.640 --> 00:43:40.220
There are several species of protea growing here

00:43:40.220 --> 00:43:44.060
and they all depend upon the arrival of seasonal fires.

00:44:13.440 --> 00:44:17.360
The fire has killed all the adult plants on this land.

00:44:17.790 --> 00:44:21.010
So this is an excellent time for seeds to germinate.

00:44:21.460 --> 00:44:23.680
There are no established competitors.

00:44:24.480 --> 00:44:28.470
In fact, it's the only time that protea seeds can germinate

00:44:28.470 --> 00:44:29.970
because these...

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:33.950
Seed heads have to be burnt before they will release their seeds.

00:44:36.160 --> 00:44:39.690
And an hour or so after scorching, the heads open.

00:45:09.530 --> 00:45:15.180
All around lies a rich ash, which will make an excellent and nutritious bed for the seeds.

00:45:17.120 --> 00:45:22.320
Lutia seeds can remain inert and apparently lifeless for many years,

00:45:22.540 --> 00:45:25.740
and then spring into life when the right conditions arrive.

00:45:26.200 --> 00:45:29.960
Not all seeds can do that. Some will die after...

00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:32.250
two or three years, no matter what the conditions.

00:45:32.670 --> 00:45:37.310
But others are able to remain alive for astonishing periods.

00:45:37.690 --> 00:45:40.080
One of the most remarkable examples

00:45:40.080 --> 00:45:43.710
comes from an archaeological site here in Japan.

00:45:43.870 --> 00:45:50.100
A thousand years ago, a small settlement

00:45:50.100 --> 00:45:53.720
with buildings like these stood at a place called Asada.

00:46:03.450 --> 00:46:08.940
The people who lived here in such houses had only just begun to master the art of working metal.

00:46:09.920 --> 00:46:14.960
They were also among the first people in Japan who knew how to plant and reap rice.

00:46:15.690 --> 00:46:18.810
And they stored their harvests in small pits in the ground.

00:46:22.040 --> 00:46:25.900
In one of those pits, they found some seeds like these.

00:46:26.710 --> 00:46:29.910
These are rice grains. They're black and obvious.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:37.590
dead. But this is a magnolia seed. Scientists took away that strange ancient

00:46:37.590 --> 00:46:46.260
seed, planted it, and it grew. At first, it looked like magnolia cobus, the wild

00:46:46.260 --> 00:46:52.470
species that still grows in Japanese woods. Then, in its tenth year, it produced

00:46:52.470 --> 00:46:58.440
its first flower buds. These, when they opened, would reveal exactly what it was.

00:47:16.220 --> 00:47:20.460
Magnolia kobus today typically has six petals on its flowers,

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:26.530
but this flower has an extra petal, seven, and this has eight.

00:47:27.480 --> 00:47:29.560
Is this a consequence of its long sleep?

00:47:30.480 --> 00:47:34.580
Or were all Magnolia cobus 2,000 years ago variable like this?

00:47:35.200 --> 00:47:44.320
Or could it be that this is an ancient species that has only survived as that one lone seed in an archaeological ruin?

00:47:44.820 --> 00:47:48.700
It's too early to know the answers to those questions, but whatever it is,

00:47:48.880 --> 00:47:54.460
this is surely a marvellous example of the fact that plants with their seeds

00:47:54.460 --> 00:47:59.960
are not only extraordinary travellers in space, but incomparable travellers in space.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:00.400
time.
